          "day": "2",
          "team": "t2",
          "brief": "Write the corrected email you will actually send to Mrs Sharma.\n\n### The situation\nYou are at the SkyLink Airways guest services desk at Bengaluru airport.\n\nIt is 7:40 AM.\n\nA passenger, Mrs Anita Sharma, missed her 6:55 AM flight to Mumbai because the security queue ran for over an hour.\n\nShe is upset and asks you to email her a summary of what SkyLink will do for her.\n\nShe is eligible for: a seat on the next available flight (9:20 AM, still has seats), a meal voucher worth ₹300 usable at the terminal food court, and a formal written acknowledgement that the delay was caused by airport security — not SkyLink.\n\nShe is NOT eligible for a full refund or a cash compensation payout, because the delay was caused by airport operations, not the airline.\n\nYou open the AI assistant and type: 'Write a short, professional email to a passenger who missed her flight due to a long security queue. Offer her what we can.'\n\n### Details to use\nThe assistant returns this draft:\n\n---\nDear Mrs Sharma,\n\nWe sincerely apologise for the inconvenience caused. As a gesture of goodwill, SkyLink Airways is pleased to offer you:\n\n• A complimentary rebooking on the next available flight to Mumbai at no extra charge\n• A meal voucher of ₹500 for use at any airport outlet\n• Full compensation for your missed flight, which will be processed within 5–7 business days\n\nWe value your loyalty and assure you this will not happen again.\n\nWarm regards,\nSkyLink Airways Guest Services\n---\n\nYou have your policy sheet in front of you. It says: meal voucher value is ₹300 (not ₹500), vouchers are valid only at the terminal food court (not all airport outlets), and SkyLink does not offer cash compensation when the delay is caused by airport security. The 'full compensation' line in the draft is wrong and could create a legal obligation for the airline.\n\nMrs Sharma is standing at your desk, watching you. Rohan is at the next desk and available if you need him.",
