          "id": "t3s053",
          "day": "2",
          "team": "t3",
          "brief": "What would you do in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nYou are at the Guest Experience desk. The desk uses a system called GuestTrack to log every visitor query. A ticket is a digital record of one guest's issue — it stays open until the issue is fully resolved.\n\nAt 10:15 a.m. a guest, Mr. Fernandes, walks up. He says he came in yesterday and reported that the lift near the food court was making a loud grinding noise. He was told it would be fixed by end of day. It has not been fixed. He is annoyed and running late.\n\nYou open GuestTrack and search for the ticket. You find it — Ticket #4412, logged yesterday at 2:08 p.m., category: Facility Fault. The status field says: CLOSED. The closing note reads: 'Guest informed. Issue escalated to Facilities.' There is no confirmation that the lift was actually repaired.\n\n### Details to use\nThe person who closed it yesterday is not on shift today. It would be easy to just open a new ticket and start fresh. But that would lose the history — and it would look like nobody dropped the ball.\n\n### Your response\nYour task: Decide what to do with Ticket #4412 right now — close it, reopen it, or open a new one — and type the update note you would enter into GuestTrack. The note should reflect what actually happened, not what looks tidiest. Then type one sentence explaining to Mr. Fernandes what you are doing and what happens next.",
          "title": "The Ticket That Wants to Stay Closed",
          "answer": "typed",
          "easier": "The ticket is still open when you find it — you only need to add an update note and tell Mr. Fernandes what happens next.",
          "fields": [],
          "harder": "Mr. Fernandes says he will write a Google review right now unless you can confirm the lift will be fixed within the hour — you cannot make that promise; your note must reflect that too.",
          "minutes": 3,
