          "team": "t3",
          "brief": "Write the one-page summary yourself — or rewrite the AI draft — so it reflects what you actually found, including the two problems.\n\n### The situation\nIt is your thirtieth day. Your supervisor has handed you a small project: produce a one-page summary of the Guest Experience desk's open and closed tickets for the past two weeks, flag anything that looks like a pattern, and recommend one change to how the team logs complaints. She will not check in — she has said the summary is yours to deliver by 4 p.m.\n\nYou pull the GuestTrack export for the last fourteen days. Here is what it shows:\n\nTotal tickets logged: 62\nClosed within 24 hours: 41\nClosed after 24 hours: 9\nStill open: 12\n\nCategories:\nFacility faults: 28\nLost property: 14\nGuest complaints (staff/service): 11\nInformation requests logged in error: 9\n\n### Details to use\nYou notice two things as you look at this. First, 9 tickets are listed as 'information requests logged in error' — these are cases where a guest just asked for directions or a store location, which does not need a ticket at all, but someone created one anyway and then marked it as an error. Second, 12 tickets are still open — but you recognise three of those ticket numbers from this week. You check them: all three were resolved verbally by your team but the status in GuestTrack was never updated to CLOSED.\n\nThe AI assistant offers to draft the summary for you. You ask it to. It gives you a draft that says: 'Overall the team is performing well, with 66% of tickets closed within 24 hours.' The maths is right but the draft does not mention the open tickets that were resolved and never updated, or the error-category problem.\n\n### Your response\nInclude your one recommendation. It should be honest, short, and useful to your supervisor.",
