          "id": "t4s067",
          "day": "1",
          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "What would you do in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nIt is your first morning at FunSphere India. Your team lead has handed you a printed Guest Entry Log for the day so far and said, 'Get familiar with this — we check it every hour.' The log is a simple table showing how many guests entered each zone of the park between 9 AM and 11 AM.\n\nHere is what the log shows:\n\nZone | 9–10 AM | 10–11 AM | Cumulative\nAdventure Rides | 210 | 180 | 390\nKids' World | 95 | 120 | 215\nFood Court | 140 | 160 | 310\nSpectacle Stage | 60 | 55 | 105\nTotal | 505 | 515 | 1,030\n\nYour job right now is to read it carefully. One of the cumulative numbers does not match the two hourly figures next to it. Find it, then write two things: which zone has the wrong cumulative, and what the correct number should be. Then write one sentence explaining why this matters for your team — what could go wrong if nobody catches it.\n\n### Details to use\nThe log itself is what matters here.",
          "title": "The Numbers That Do Not Add Up",
          "answer": "typed",
          "easier": "Tell the student that exactly one cumulative is wrong before they begin.",
          "fields": [],
          "harder": "Add a second small error in the hourly figures themselves and ask the student to find both.",
          "minutes": 3,
          "support": "assistant",
          "criteria": [
            "Correctly identifies Kids' World as the zone with the error (95 + 120 = 215 is actually correct — student should identify Food Court: 140 + 160 = 300, not 310)",
            "States the correct cumulative for Food Court as 300",
            "Explains a real consequence — such as overcapacity decisions or revenue counts being wrong — not just 'it will cause problems'",
            "Reading data"
          ],
          "measures": [
            "Clear communication"
          ],
          "needsImage": false
