          "brief": "What would you write or record for this task?\n\n\n### The situation\nIt is day thirty at FunSphere India. Your team lead has handed you a week's worth of guest experience data and said: 'You are running Monday's briefing. Tell the team what the numbers show and what we should do differently next week. Nobody will check your slides before the meeting.'\n\nHere is the data for the past seven days:\n\nDay | Total Guests | Complaints Logged | Avg Wait Time (mins) | Upsell Offers Made | Upsell Offers Accepted\nMon | 2,100 | 8 | 18 | 210 | 63\nTue | 1,850 | 5 | 15 | 185 | 74\nWed | 1,950 | 12 | 22 | 195 | 39\nThu | 2,050 | 6 | 17 | 205 | 82\nFri | 2,800 | 18 | 31 | 280 | 56\nSat | 4,200 | 34 | 44 | 420 | 63\nSun | 3,900 | 29 | 41 | 390 | 58\n\n### Details to use\nYou need to produce the briefing note itself — not a description of what you would write, but the actual document. It should cover: the one metric that shows the clearest problem and what the numbers say specifically, the day where something looks unexpectedly off compared to the rest of the week, and two concrete things the team should try next week. Keep it to one page — your colleagues will read it in two minutes at 9 AM before the park opens.\n\nNo support is available. Write the briefing note.",
          "title": "Your First Solo Review",
