          "id": "t4s076",
          "day": "12",
          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "What do you do, and what exactly do you type if you log anything?\n\n### The situation\nFunSphere India's policy is that every guest complaint — whether it comes in by email, at a counter, or during a walkabout — must be logged in GuestTrack before the end of the shift it was received. A logged complaint gets a reference number. An unlogged one does not exist in the record.\n\nYesterday was not your shift. Today you are covering the guest services counter and you overhear your colleague Priya telling another associate: 'That lady who came in at 5pm yesterday — the one upset about the broken fountain in the kids' zone — I told her I'd pass it on, but I forgot to log it.'\n\nPriya is now on break and has left the desk. The guest has not called back.\n\nYou check GuestTrack. There is no complaint logged for a broken fountain, and no guest query from yesterday evening matching that description.\n\n### Details to use\nYou have the following options in GuestTrack: log a new 'Retrospective Guest Complaint' (which asks for the date the complaint was received, the original associate's name, and the details known), escalate to your shift supervisor, or do nothing and wait for Priya to return.\n\n### Your response\nExplain your choice, then write the log entry if you make one.",
          "title": "The Walk-In That Was Never Logged",
          "answer": "typed",
          "easier": "Priya has left you a sticky note with the guest's first name and a rough description of what she said.",
          "fields": [],
          "harder": "Your shift supervisor is in a meeting and you have a queue at the counter; you must decide and log without being able to ask anyone.",
          "minutes": 4,
          "support": "assistant",
