          "brief": "Speak your actual pitch to Rajiv aloud — sixty seconds maximum.\n\n### The situation\nIt is a Saturday, 10:40 a.m., your third day at FunSphere India. The park opens fully in twenty minutes and you are already handling a short queue at the Guest Experience counter. A family of four — father Rajiv, mother, and two children aged about eight and eleven — are renewing their day tickets at the desk. They bought standard entry at ₹1,200 per adult and ₹900 per child. Total paid: ₹4,200.\n\nRajiv mentions that his younger daughter really wants to ride the Cyclone Coaster, but he has just seen a sign saying it has a 45-minute wait from park open. He looks frustrated.\n\n### Details to use\nYou know from your induction that FunSphere India offers a FastTrack wristband — ₹500 per person — which lets guests skip to the front of the queue on six major rides, including the Cyclone Coaster. There is also a family FastTrack bundle at ₹1,600 for up to four people, which works out cheaper than buying four individually.\n\nYou open the AI assistant and type: 'A family of four want to avoid the long Cyclone Coaster wait. Write me a short pitch for the FastTrack wristband.'\n\nThe assistant returns:\n\n---\n'Great news — our FastTrack wristband gets you to the front of every ride in the park, all day, for just ₹400 per person. For a family of four that's only ₹1,600 total — brilliant value!'\n---\n\nThere are two people waiting behind Rajiv's family. You have about ninety seconds to make the offer, and you want to get it right.\n\n### Your response\nBefore you start, identify what is wrong in the AI's draft and make sure your pitch does not repeat it. Your pitch should give Rajiv a clear reason to say yes, state the correct price, and not oversell what the product does.",
