          "brief": "What would you say in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nIt is the morning of your third day at Suraksha Life Insurance. Before you start calling leads, your manager sends the team a daily call report from yesterday. It shows each executive's numbers across four columns: calls attempted, calls connected, policies discussed, and policies sold.\n\nHere is the report:\n\n| Executive | Calls attempted | Calls connected | Policies discussed | Policies sold |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Priya | 20 | 14 | 10 | 3 |\n| Rohan | 20 | 15 | 6 | 4 |\n| Deepa | 20 | 13 | 12 | 2 |\n| Team average | 20 | 14 | 9.3 | 3 |\n\nYour manager has asked each of you to look at the report and come to the huddle ready to say: which one number across the whole table looks most out of place, and what question it raises.\n\n### Details to use\nThis is not about judging a colleague. It is about reading a set of numbers carefully before the day starts. Speak your answer aloud as if you are at the huddle — one minute, no notes.",
          "title": "Reading a daily call report before you pick up the phone",
