          "team": "t3",
          "brief": "What would you write or record for this task?\n\n\n### The situation\nYou have a 15-minute call booked for 11 am with Ramesh Iyer, 52, a self-employed accountant. His details are in the CRM: he has had a Suraksha health rider for four years, his son just started college, and he mentioned last month that his business partner recently died without life cover — leaving the partner's family with nothing. Your manager wants you to explore whether Ramesh might want to top up his coverage.\n\nBefore the call you ask the AI assistant: 'Give me a quick call plan for Ramesh Iyer — 52, self-employed, has our health rider, son in college, business partner recently died without cover. Goal is to explore life cover top-up.'\n\nThe assistant replies:\n\n---\nCall plan for Ramesh:\n1. Open warmly — ask how the family is settling into the new college routine.\n2. Acknowledge the business partner situation — say something like 'I heard about your colleague, I hope his family is managing.'\n3. Bridge to cover: 'That kind of situation makes people think about their own arrangements — have you reviewed yours recently?'\n4. Pitch SurakshaShield Term Plan — ideal for his age group, premiums from ₹400/month for ₹50 lakh cover.\n5. Close: ask if he wants a quote sent by end of day.\n---\n\nTwo things about this plan need your attention before you dial:\n\n### Details to use\nFirst: The ₹400/month figure for a 52-year-old, ₹50 lakh term plan is wrong. Your rate card (pinned above your desk) shows the base premium for a 52-year-old non-smoker at that cover level is ₹1,850/month. Mentioning ₹400 on the call would be a mis-selling risk.\n\nSecond: Step 2 tells you to say 'I heard about your colleague' — but Ramesh told this to a previous executive at Suraksha, not to you. If you say it that way, Ramesh may feel his private conversation was passed around without care.\n\nArjun is at his desk. Write the revised call plan you will actually use — same five-step structure is fine, but fix both problems. Then write two sentences: one on the premium error and one on the phrasing problem, explaining what you changed and why.",
