          "id": "t4s079",
          "day": "3",
          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "Write the corrected call plan you will actually use.\n\n### The situation\nIt is day three at Sahayog Finserve. Part of your job as a Relationship Executive is preparing short call plans before you phone customers — a few bullet points covering what the customer currently has, what you want to offer, and the main objection you expect so you are not caught off guard.\n\nYou have a call booked at 11 a.m. with a customer named Rajan Shetty. His file shows:\n- Active loan: ₹15,000 group loan, 8 of 24 instalments paid\n- Occupation listed: vegetable vendor, Dadar market\n- Last contact: 4 months ago, no issues\n\nYou ask the AI assistant to help you prepare. You type: \"Customer is a vegetable vendor with a ₹15,000 group loan, 8 of 24 instalments paid. Suggest a call plan to offer him a new individual loan of ₹30,000.\"\n\nThe assistant replies:\n\n### Details to use\n---\n\"Call plan for Rajan:\n1. Open by thanking him for 16 on-time payments.\n2. Mention that he is more than halfway through his loan.\n3. Explain the individual loan product — higher amount, flexible repayment.\n4. If he hesitates, point out that seasonal vendors often need working capital before the monsoon.\n5. Close by asking him to visit the branch this week.\"\n---\n\nRead the call plan against Rajan's file. Two things the assistant says do not match the actual numbers. If you follow this plan on the call, Rajan will hear something wrong about his own account — and he will notice.\n\n### Your response\nFix the factual errors, keep anything in the draft that is genuinely useful, and add one point of your own about how you will handle it if Rajan says he cannot afford a bigger EMI right now.",
