          "brief": "Decide what Dinesh does first, and in what order the two tasks get completed.\n\n### The situation\nIt is 14:00 on day three. The hub has one operational forklift today – the second is out for a service. Two things are happening at once:\n\n**Bay 1:** A truck carrying 15 pallets of tower hardware for the Nagpur site has just docked. The driver has a hard deadline to leave by 14:45 or he goes into overtime, which ConnectCore pays. Unloading needs the forklift.\n\n**Bay 4:** The 16:00 outbound van for the Mumbai priority route is being loaded. It carries fibre patch panels ordered by a hospital network upgrade – flagged as Priority 1 in the system. The van driver says the load must be done by 15:30 to make the traffic window. Loading needs the forklift.\n\nBoth bay team leads are asking you for the forklift. You have no extra staff. The forklift operator is Dinesh, and he can only be in one place.\n\n### Details to use\nYou cannot call your shift manager – she is in a safety audit and has said no interruptions except emergencies.\n\n### Your response\nSpeak your answer out loud as if you are explaining it to Dinesh and the two bay team leads at the same time. You have sixty seconds.",
          "title": "Two bays need the same forklift at the same time",
          "answer": "spoken",
          "easier": "Tell the student that Priority 1 items always take precedence over cost-saving considerations at ConnectCore.",
          "fields": [],
          "harder": "Add that Dinesh says he needs a five-minute break – his last break was four hours ago and policy requires a break every four hours. The student must fold this in without losing either deadline.",
