          "day": "2",
          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "What do you do next?\n\n### The situation\nIt is your second morning at ConnectCore Supply Chain. You are getting used to the hub's daily dispatch dashboard. Each row shows one outbound run: the vehicle ID, the load count (number of items loaded), the scan count (items that have been scanned at the bay door as they went onto the van), and a status flag — either CLEAR or CHECK.\n\nHere is today's dashboard for the first four runs:\n\nRun | Vehicle | Load count | Scan count | Flag\nR-01 | Van 04   | 24         | 24         | CLEAR\nR-02 | Van 11   | 18         | 16         | CHECK\nR-03 | Van 07   | 31         | 31         | CLEAR\nR-04 | Van 19   | 9          | 9          | CLEAR\n\nVan 11 carries antenna mounting kits destined for a tower site in Nashik. The driver, Dinesh, has already left.\n\n### Details to use\nYou paste the dashboard into the AI assistant and ask: 'Summarise what needs attention this morning.' The assistant replies:\n\n---\nGood morning. Based on the dashboard, all four runs are clear to proceed. Run R-02 on Van 11 shows a CHECK flag, but the two-item discrepancy is within the acceptable variance for a load of this size. No immediate action is required.\n---\n\nNo one told you there is an 'acceptable variance' rule at ConnectCore. Meena is at her desk.\n\n### Your response\nDescribe your immediate actions — who you contact, what you check, and what you do about the AI's summary. Write your answer as a short set of steps in the order you would take them.",
          "title": "The assistant reads the dashboard wrong — and you have to spot it",
