          "brief": "What would you do in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nIt is your third day at Swiftstore Logistics. Every morning the operations team checks a daily dispatch summary before the floor briefing. Priya has just handed you today's sheet and said, 'Have a look and flag anything odd — I'll be right back.'\n\nHere is the summary for Zone B, yesterday's evening shift (6 p.m. – 10 p.m.):\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Orders assigned to pickers | 214 |\n| Orders picked and confirmed in system | 189 |\n| Orders handed to dispatch | 203 |\n| Orders marked 'Dispatched' in system | 189 |\n| Pending returns logged | 4 |\n\nYou are not sure what to make of the gap, so you ask the AI assistant: 'This dispatch summary shows 203 orders handed to dispatch but only 189 marked Dispatched in the system. What are the normal reasons for this gap and what should I do?'\n\nThe assistant replies:\n\n### Details to use\n'The most likely explanation is a system sync delay. Dispatch systems often update in batches overnight. By the time you read this, the numbers will almost certainly have corrected themselves. No action is needed unless the gap persists for more than 48 hours.'\n\nYou look at the sheet again. The shift ended at 10 p.m. yesterday. It is now 9 a.m. — eleven hours later. The system number has not changed. You also notice something the assistant did not mention: the number of orders handed to dispatch (203) is actually higher than the number picked and confirmed (189). Physically, that should not be possible.\n\nIn the text box below, write what you would say to Priya when she comes back. Tell her: (1) the specific numbers that concern you, (2) why the assistant's 'wait 48 hours' advice does not feel right here, and (3) what you think should be checked on the floor before the briefing.",
          "title": "The Dashboard Number That Does Not Add Up",
