          "day": "1",
          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "Look at each row.\n\n### The situation\nIt is your first morning at Medicare Lifeline. You are on the shop floor of the medical supplies store. Sunita has shown you the daily stock sheet — a printed list that staff fill in every morning by physically counting items on the shelves and writing the number next to each product.\n\nHere is today's sheet for the Wound Care section:\n\n| Product | Yesterday's closing count | Units received overnight | Units sold yesterday | Today's expected count | Today's actual count |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sterile gauze 10x10 | 48 | 24 | 15 | 57 | 57 |\n| Adhesive bandage rolls | 32 | 0 | 8 | 24 | 24 |\n| Antiseptic wipes (box) | 20 | 12 | 6 | 26 | 19 |\n| Elastic crepe bandage | 15 | 0 | 3 | 12 | 12 |\n\n### Details to use\nThe formula is simple: Yesterday's closing + received overnight − sold yesterday = today's expected count. The actual count is what the staff member physically counted this morning.\n\n### Your response\nOne product's actual count does not match its expected count and the gap is large enough that it cannot be rounding. Write:\n1. Which product has the mismatch and what the gap is.\n2. Two possible reasons this could have happened — think about what actually goes wrong in a medical supplies shop.\n3. What you would do right now, on your first day, before flagging it to anyone.",
          "title": "Morning stock sheet, one number that does not add up",
          "answer": "typed",
          "easier": "Tell the student which row to focus on and ask them only what the gap is and one reason it could have happened.",
          "fields": [],
