          "brief": "Write:\n1.\n\n### The situation\nYou are twelve days into your role at Medicare Lifeline. One of your responsibilities is to check the customer service ticket tracker every morning — a shared spreadsheet where any complaint or query gets logged with a ticket number, the customer's name, the issue, the date it was raised, and the status.\n\nHere is today's view of open tickets:\n\n### Details to use\n| Ticket # | Customer | Issue | Date raised | Status | Last updated |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CL-041 | Mr. Ashok Rao | Wrong item delivered — ordered L-size BP cuff, received M-size | 14 Jan | Awaiting exchange | 14 Jan |\n| CL-044 | Mrs. Priya Shetty | Wheelchair wheel loose — safety concern raised | 15 Jan | Escalated to service team | 15 Jan |\n| CL-047 | Mr. Deven Nair | Refund request — glucometer returned within policy window | 17 Jan | Processing | 20 Jan |\n| CL-051 | Ms. Fatima Qureshi | Complained staff were rude at counter | 19 Jan | Under review | 19 Jan |\n| CL-053 | Mr. Harish Goel | Blood pressure monitor not switching on — within warranty | 21 Jan | Awaiting callback from customer | 21 Jan |\n\nToday's date is 23 January.\n\nLook at the tracker carefully. Two tickets stand out as needing immediate attention — not because the issue is the most dramatic, but because of what the dates and status together are telling you.\n\n### Your response\nWhich two tickets would you act on first today, and why — be specific about what the dates and status are telling you.\n2. For one of those two tickets, write the exact words you would say when you call or message the customer to follow up.\n3. After you follow up, what do you update in the tracker so the next person who opens it knows exactly where things stand?",
