          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "What does the Saturday number tell you in plain terms — how many customers walked in and left without buying anything, compared to a typical day?\n\n### The situation\nIt is day eighteen at Medicare Lifeline. Your supervisor has shared this week's footfall and sales conversion report and asked you to look it over before the team briefing.\n\nThe report covers Monday to Saturday. 'Footfall' means the number of customers who walked into the store. 'Transactions' means the number of customers who actually bought something. 'Conversion rate' is transactions divided by footfall, as a percentage.\n\n| Day | Footfall | Transactions | Conversion rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Monday | 112 | 89 | 79% |\n| Tuesday | 98 | 77 | 79% |\n| Wednesday | 104 | 82 | 79% |\n| Thursday | 118 | 93 | 79% |\n| Friday | 126 | 99 | 79% |\n| Saturday | 141 | 68 | 48% |\n\nEvery day except one sits at exactly 79% conversion. Saturday is 48%.\n\n### Details to use\nA consistent 79% across five different days, with different footfall numbers, is itself unusual — real numbers vary a little. But your job right now is to deal with the Saturday figure.\n\n### Your response\nWrite:\n1. \n2. Think of three specific things that could cause a single-day drop this sharp in a medical supplies store — not generic reasons, but things that could actually happen here.\n3. What one question would you ask the Saturday floor staff before the briefing, and why that question and not another?",
          "title": "Weekly footfall and conversion numbers, one day is an outlier",
