          "brief": "Write your note to your supervisor.\n\n### The situation\nIt is your thirtieth day at Medicare Lifeline. Nobody is checking on you today. Your supervisor has left you a note: 'Month-end dashboard is ready. Have a look and tell me by 5pm what we need to fix in the next two weeks and why. It is your call.'\n\nHere is the dashboard for January:\n\n**Sales**\n- Monthly revenue target: ₹8,40,000\n- Actual revenue: ₹7,63,000\n- Gap: ₹77,000 (9.2% below target)\n\n**Stock**\n- Stockout incidents logged (times a customer asked for something we did not have): 34\n- Of those 34: 19 were in the Chronic Care category (BP monitors, glucometers, diabetes supplies)\n- Average time to restock after a stockout: 4.1 days\n\n**Customer feedback**\n- Complaints received: 11\n- Of those 11: 7 mentioned 'wait time at counter'\n- Net score from end-of-visit feedback slips: 61 out of 100\n\n### Details to use\n**Staff**\n- Shifts covered as scheduled: 88%\n- Shifts where one person covered two roles: 9 occasions\n\nYou also have access to the AI assistant. It drafts the following when you ask it to summarise the top priority:\n\n*'The top priority is customer wait times. Seven of eleven complaints mention this, and reducing queue time will directly lift the net score from 61 to above 80, which is the industry benchmark.'\n*\n\nBefore you write your note to your supervisor, consider whether the assistant's summary is reliable — it has picked one thing and made a confident prediction (score will go above 80). Ask yourself what it is not seeing.\n\n### Your response\nIt should:\n1. Name the one or two things you would fix first and explain why — using the actual numbers.\n2. Say where you disagree with the AI summary or where it has missed something, and why.\n3. Say what you need (information, people, time) to get started — nobody will offer it, so you have to say it.",
