          "brief": "Write a one-page plan (roughly 250–350 words) for Monday's ops team.\n\n### The situation\nIt is your thirtieth day at Swifthaul Logistics Pvt Ltd. Your manager, Kavitha, has handed you a project with no detailed instructions. She said: 'Look at this week's exception data and tell me what we should do differently next week. Write me something I can share with the ops team on Monday. Nobody will check your work before Monday morning — if you need something, ask for it.'\n\nHere is the exception log for the week of 17–21 June, across all routes:\n\n### Details to use\n| Exception Type              | Mon | Tue | Wed | Thu | Fri | Total | % of Daily Dispatches (avg 380/day) |\n|-----------------------------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-------|--------------------------------------|\n| Address not found           | 12  | 9   | 11  | 14  | 17  | 63    | 3.3%                                 |\n| Customer not available      | 8   | 7   | 10  | 9   | 6   | 40    | 2.1%                                 |\n| Vehicle breakdown           | 1   | 0   | 3   | 1   | 2   | 7     | 0.4%                                 |\n| Parcel damaged in transit   | 2   | 4   | 2   | 3   | 5   | 16    | 0.8%                                 |\n| Driver called in sick       | 0   | 1   | 0   | 2   | 1   | 4     | 0.2%                                 |\n| Wrong parcel loaded on van  | 5   | 3   | 6   | 4   | 8   | 26    | 1.4%                                 |\n\nAdditional context:\n- Friday numbers are consistently higher across most categories.\n- The 'Address not found' count has been rising each day this week.\n- Swifthaul's internal target is to keep total exceptions below 6% of daily dispatches. This week's average is 8.2%.\n- There is no current process for pre-checking addresses before the driver leaves the hub.\n\n### Your response\nIt should:\n1. Say which two exception types are the biggest problem and why — use the numbers.\n2. Suggest one concrete action for each that Swifthaul could start next week.\n3. Note what you do not know yet and what information you would need before being fully confident in your suggestions.",
          "title": "Design next week's exception-handling plan from this week's data",
