          "day": "2",
          "team": "t3",
          "brief": "What would you do in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nYou are two days in at Brindavan Auto Components, helping with daily operations from a shared desk near the dispatch bay.\n\nYour job includes keeping the dispatch tracker up to date — a shared spreadsheet where each row is one outbound order.\n\nToday's row for Order #BAC-1047 (200 pressed steel brackets, customer: Mehta Assemblies) shows Status: 'Pending' and Scheduled Dispatch: 'Today 16:00'.\n\nIt is 10:30 am.\n\nThe dispatch supervisor, Rajan, walks past and mentions — offhand, on his way to the canteen — that the BAC-1047 truck actually went out at 8:15 am this morning because the driver had another stop and needed an early start.\n\nHe did not update the tracker.\n\nHe does not stop to help you do it.\n\nThe tracker fields you need to fill in are: Status, Actual Dispatch Time, Notes, and Updated By.\n\n### Details to use\nThe problem is that 'Pending' is the safe default — if you change it and something is wrong, the error is now attached to your name.\n\nIt would be easier to leave it.\n\nBut if Mehta Assemblies calls to ask where their order is, whoever picks up that call will tell them it has not left yet.\n\n### Your response\nType what you would update each tracker field to, and in one or two sentences say why you are doing it now rather than waiting for Rajan to do it himself.",
