          "id": "t3s055",
          "day": "8",
          "team": "t3",
          "brief": "What would you do in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nBrindavan Auto Components uses a simple helpdesk-style ticketing tool for customer complaints and follow-ups.\n\nTicket #CX-084 was raised five days ago by Prakash Motors, who received 30 engine mounts with the wrong part code printed on the outer carton — the parts themselves are correct, but the label says a different code.\n\nThe previous status note on the ticket reads: 'Label issue confirmed.\n\nCorrected cartons to be sent by courier.\n\nTicket closed.' Status is marked 'Resolved'.\n\nThis morning Prakash Motors emails again: the corrected cartons never arrived.\n\nYou check with the dispatch team.\n\nThe replacement cartons were printed but never actually shipped — the courier booking was not made.\n\nSo the ticket was marked 'Resolved' before the resolution actually happened.\n\nNow you need to reopen it and update the record honestly.\n\n### Details to use\nThe problem: the person who closed it early is a senior colleague, Dinesh, and reopening it creates a visible record that his closure was premature.\n\nIt would be easier to open a new ticket and say nothing.\n\n### Your response\nWrite what you would update on Ticket #CX-084 — the new status, a new status note, and a one-paragraph message to Prakash Motors — and in two sentences explain your decision to reopen the original ticket rather than start a fresh one.",
          "title": "The Ticket That Says 'Resolved' But Isn't",
