          "day": "30",
          "team": "t3",
          "brief": "What would you write or record for this task?\n\n\n### The situation\nIt is your thirtieth day at Brindavan Auto Components. Your manager Priya has handed you a small project: prepare a one-page operations note for next week's review meeting that covers open exceptions — anything that is unresolved or at risk — across dispatch, inbound stock, and customer complaints. She will not check your work before the meeting. You pull three reports from the system:\n\nDISPATCH: 4 orders overdue by more than 2 days. System status on all four: 'In Progress'. No notes on any of them.\n\nINBOUND STOCK: 2 purchase orders showing 'Received — Complete' in the system. One of these is PO# BAC-IN-302 (connecting rod blanks) — you flagged a physical count discrepancy on this ten days ago but the system record was never formally corrected. The flag you added is still there as a note, but the status still says Complete.\n\n### Details to use\nCUSTOMER COMPLAINTS: 7 open tickets. Two of them have not been updated in more than 7 days. Ticket #CX-101 (Rajdhani Vehicles) shows 'Closed' in the system — but you know from your own work that the credit note was still outstanding as of last week. You do not know if it was issued since.\n\nBefore you write the operations note, you need to decide: which of these records must be corrected in the system before the note goes to the meeting, and which can you report as exceptions without first fixing the record? Then write the one-page note as an artefact — plain text, clear headings, honest about what is unresolved and what the system shows versus what is actually known. Every number and status in the final note must reflect what is true, not what the system currently says.",
          "title": "The Backlog That the System Does Not Show",
          "answer": "artefact",
          "easier": "Priya gives you a template with three sections already labelled. Fill it in honestly, focusing on what to say about the two records you know are wrong.",
          "fields": [
            "Which records you correct in the system before writing the note, and why",
            "Which exceptions you report without correcting, and why",
            "The one-page operations note itself"
          ],
          "harder": "The meeting is in 90 minutes and you cannot reach any of the people who own the open dispatch orders. Write the note so it is honest about the gap in information without making the team look like they have lost control.",
          "minutes": 5,
          "support": "assistant",
          "criteria": [
            "Identifies that Ticket #CX-101 being marked 'Closed' is the most urgent record correction — a closed ticket that may not be resolved is the highest risk if the customer calls",
            "Does not present the inbound stock PO as 'Complete' in the note without flagging the unresolved count discrepancy from ten days ago",
            "The operations note distinguishes clearly between 'what the system shows' and 'what is actually known' for at least two of the three areas",
            "Note is actionable — each exception includes what needs to happen next and who needs to act, not just a list of problems"
          ],
          "measures": [
            "Planning and organising",
            "Problem solving",
            "Follow-through",
            "Clear communication"
          ],
