          "brief": "What would you do in this situation?\n\n\n### The situation\nIt is your third day at Brindavan Auto Components. Part of your morning routine is checking the daily dispatch tracker — a simple shared sheet that shows how many units of each part code left the warehouse the previous day.\n\nHere is yesterday's data from the tracker:\n\n| Part Code | Units Dispatched | Customer |\n|-----------|-----------------|----------|\n| BAC-112   | 200             | Fenix Assemblies |\n| BAC-119   | 150             | Raj Motors |\n| BAC-204   | 80              | Coromandel Parts |\n| BAC-331   | 320             | Fenix Assemblies |\n| BAC-119   | 50              | Coromandel Parts |\n\nNote: BAC-119 appears twice because two separate orders went out on the same day to different customers.\n\n### Details to use\nYou paste this data into the AI assistant and ask it to write a one-paragraph summary for your manager's morning briefing — something short that covers total units dispatched and flags anything worth noting.\n\nThe assistant returns this:\n\n---\n\"Yesterday Brindavan Auto Components dispatched a total of 750 units across four customers. The largest single dispatch was BAC-331 to Fenix Assemblies at 320 units. Part code BAC-119 moved to two customers — 150 units to Raj Motors and 50 units to Coromandel Parts, totalling 200 units. No anomalies noted.\"\n---\n\nCheck the assistant's summary against the tracker numbers yourself.\n\nThen do two things:\n1. Write the corrected paragraph — the one you would actually send to your manager.\n2. In one sentence, explain what the AI got wrong and why it matters in an operations context.",
