          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "write the replenishment requests for the items that actually need one.\n\n### The situation\nThe pharmacy team at Meditrust Hospitals sends a daily stock alert sheet to operations each afternoon. If any item is below its minimum level, operations is supposed to raise a replenishment request with the central supply coordinator before 5 p.m.\n\nToday's sheet:\n\nItem | Current stock | Minimum level | Last replenishment | Alert\nParacetamol 500mg tabs | 3,200 | 1,000 | 12 days ago | None\nSodium Chloride 0.9% IV bags | 140 | 200 | 6 days ago | BELOW MINIMUM\nOmeprazole 20mg caps | 850 | 500 | 9 days ago | None\nSurgical spirit 450ml | 38 | 40 | 21 days ago | BELOW MINIMUM\nAmoxicillin 500mg caps | 600 | 600 | 3 days ago | None\nAzithromycin 500mg tabs | 220 | 300 | 4 days ago | None\n\n### Details to use\nThe system has flagged two items: Sodium Chloride IV bags and Surgical spirit. But look more carefully. Azithromycin shows 220 in stock against a minimum of 300 and has not been flagged — the system has missed it. Surgical spirit's last replenishment was 21 days ago, which is also longer than every other item on the list.\n\n### Your response\nFor each, state the item name, the current stock, the minimum level, and whether you are raising it because the system flagged it or because you spotted it yourself. Then write one sentence explaining what you would tell the supply coordinator about the Azithromycin.",
          "title": "Pharmacy stock sheet — two problems, one obvious and one not",
          "answer": "artefact",
          "easier": "Add a bold line in the brief: 'Check every row yourself — the system does not always catch everything.'",
