          "day": "2",
          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "Update the ticket.\n\n### The situation\nYou are working in the Operations Coordinator team, which uses a system called OpsDesk to track every service request raised by hospital departments. A ticket is a record of one request — it has a status, a note log, and a name attached to it.\n\nYou are reviewing the open-ticket list as part of your morning check. One ticket catches your eye:\n\nTicket #OD-1847\nRaised by: Housekeeping, Ward 6\nRequest: Replace broken IV-trolley wheel, patient safety risk\nStatus: RESOLVED\nLast note (added yesterday, 4:52 pm): 'Maintenance informed. Will action.'\n\nYou walk past Ward 6 on your way to get water. The trolley is still there, wheel still broken, a nurse has taped a handwritten 'DO NOT USE' sign to it.\n\n### Details to use\nThe ticket says resolved. The trolley says otherwise. Changing the status back to open means the person who closed it will get a flag on their name. Leaving it means the record is wrong and the safety issue is invisible to anyone checking the system.\n\n### Your response\nWrite the note you would add to the log — it should say exactly what you saw, when, and what the status needs to be, without blaming anyone by name.",
          "title": "The Ticket That Says 'Resolved' But Isn't",
          "answer": "artefact",
          "easier": "The ticket was only closed ten minutes ago and the same colleague is still at their desk — you can check with them first.",
          "fields": [
            "Ticket note to add to OD-1847"
          ],
          "harder": "The ticket was closed by the department head, not a peer, and the department head is currently in a board meeting — you still have to update it.",
          "minutes": 3,
          "support": "assistant",
          "criteria": [
            "Changes the status back to open, not left as resolved",
            "Note includes what was physically observed and when",
            "Does not name or blame the colleague who closed the ticket",
            "Note is specific enough that someone reading it later knows exactly what still needs to happen"
          ],
          "measures": [
            "Follow-through",
            "Clear communication",
            "Problem solving"
          ],
          "needsImage": false
        },
