          "team": "t4",
          "brief": "Edit the AI's draft into a reply you can actually send.\n\n### The situation\nYou are two days into your role as Operations Coordinator at Meditrust Hospitals. Part of your job is handling incoming queries from patients and referring departments that come into the shared ops inbox.\n\nThis morning, a message arrived from a patient, Mrs. Rekha Shenoy, who had a knee replacement surgery here last week. She has written:\n\n\"I was told I would receive a follow-up call from the physiotherapy team within 48 hours of discharge. I was discharged on Monday morning. It is now Wednesday and nobody has called. I am in pain and I do not know what exercises I should be doing. Please help.\"\n\nYou do not yet know the physio team's current call-back timelines, and you have not worked with them before. You open the AI assistant to draft a reply. The assistant produces this:\n\n---\n\"Dear Mrs. Shenoy,\n\n### Details to use\nThank you for reaching out to Meditrust Hospitals. We sincerely apologise for the delay in your follow-up call. I can confirm that a physiotherapist will call you today before 5 PM. Please keep your phone available.\n\nWe also recommend that in the meantime you begin gentle heel-slide exercises and straight-leg raises, which are standard post-knee-replacement exercises that should be safe to start.\n\nWarm regards,\nMeditrust Hospitals Operations Team\"\n---\n\nYou have not spoken to the physio team. You do not know if they can call today before 5 PM. You do not have Mrs. Shenoy's discharge notes or her surgeon's instructions.\n\n### Your response\nYou must not make any promise about timing that you have not confirmed. You must not give any exercise instructions, because you do not have her clinical notes. The reply should still feel warm and reassuring — Mrs. Shenoy is in pain and worried. Keep it under 120 words.",
